      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning, Viktor.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'good' straight to 'Viktor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Доброе утро.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Good morning.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'good' directly with 'morning', combining 'good' with 'morning'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Виктор приготовил такой омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Victor prepared such an omelet.' — Notice the proper name meaning 'Viktor' paired with the verb for 'cooked'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Это типа извинения?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this a kind of apology?' Lyova turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'guy', followed by 'apologies'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это типа омлет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is a kind of omelet.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'this' directly with 'omelette'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ешь давай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on, eat.' Chef Barinov leads with the direct command form meaning 'eat', followed by 'let's'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я опоздаю на работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will be late for work.' Chef Barinov uses the perfective verb meaning 'to be late' to focus on the single completed result, pairing it with 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Да, кушайте быстрее.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, eat faster.' the speaker leads with the direct command form meaning 'eat', followed by 'faster'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "Ладно, я съем это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Alright, I will eat this.' Lyova delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to eat up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это очень вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is very delicious.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'very much' directly with 'it's tasty', combining 'very much' with 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Нам пора идти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is time for us to go.' The phrasing opens with 'it's time' before bringing in 'to go' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
